B = [
('Wajima-nuri Lacquer Houses','East Asia','Edo-present','lacquerware','In the style of Wajima-nuri Lacquer Houses, thick, glassy, flawless coats of black and deep vermilion lacquer polished to a mirror depth with a warm inner glow. Forms are crisp, perfectly even and substantial, with sturdy rounded edges and neat raised foot rings, because the hard foundation shows only at the contours. Red interiors contrast with black exteriors. Decoration is restrained and rare, fine gold powder painting of tiny flowers, birds and grasses in sparse arrangements, or hair-thin carved lines filled with gold leaf. Large areas stay plain and unbroken. The mood is formal, luxurious, calm and exact.'),
('Kamakura-bori','East Asia','Kamakura-present','woodcraft','In the style of Kamakura-bori, simple solid wooden forms such as discs, rounded rectangles and lobed outlines with deep carved rims, covered in bold shallow gouge carving. Flowers, leaves and waves are cut with rounded petal edges and flat bevelled facets, leaving visible chisel marks. The lacquer is slightly matte, a vermilion or cinnabar layer over black that wears through at the high points to reveal a warm brown underlayer, so edges look softly aged. Colours stay in red-brown, black and warm wood tones. Folk-craft, sculptural, handmade and quietly rustic.'),
('Iznik Ceramic Workshops','Middle East','16th century','ceramics','In the style of Iznik Ceramic Workshops, bright white ground painted under a brilliant clear glaze with vivid fresh colour. The palette is cobalt blue, turquoise, emerald green and a thick, slightly raised tomato red, all drawn crisply with fine black outlines. Patterns are stylised garden flowers such as tulips, carnations and hyacinths, with long curling feathery leaves and cloud bands, arranged symmetrically in flowing arabesques that fill the surface and run around borders and arches. Surfaces are glossy, crisp and fresh, with rhythmic, balanced repetition that feels like a flowering garden.'),
('Zildjian Cymbal Foundry','Middle East','17th century-present','musical instruments','In the style of Zildjian Cymbal Foundry, hand-hammered bronze with a bright warm gold sheen, circular and gently dished with a raised central dome. The surface shows concentric lathe grooves and dense dimpled hammer marks, with darker burnished tones in the recesses and polished highlights on the ridges. Older looks are dark, brown and dull gold, newer looks are brilliantly polished and mirror bright. Edges are thin and slightly wavy. A single small printed stamp with an ornate logo in black or gold ink sits near the centre, and a small round hole pierces the middle. Warm, metallic, tactile and handmade.'),
('Tabriz Carpet Workshops','Middle East','16th-20th century','textiles','In the style of Tabriz Carpet Workshops, finely knotted wool and silk surfaces with a large central medallion on an open or dense field, framed by a wide main border and several narrow guard borders. Colours are rich and layered, ivory, deep madder red, indigo, navy, soft turquoise and gold. Motifs are curling vines, palmettes, trees, vases of flowers and cartouches, drawn in very precise, curved lines, with quarter medallions echoing in the corners. The pile is short and velvety with sharply defined detail, strict symmetry and a spacious, courtly composition.'),
('Kashan Carpet Workshops','Middle East','19th-20th century','textiles','In the style of Kashan Carpet Workshops, closely knotted surfaces with a velvet-soft short pile and a slightly glossy sheen, in a deep luxurious palette of claret and ruby red, midnight blue and ivory. A large lobed central medallion with pendants floats on a dense field of scrolling vines, palmettes and arabesque blossoms, framed by a wide border of repeating floral cartouches. Corners repeat quarter medallions. Fine ivory outlines define stems and leaves. Detail is tiny and exact, symmetry is perfect, and the mood is formal, rich and elegant.'),
('Fes Zellij Workshops','Africa','Marinid-present','tilework','In the style of Fes Zellij Workshops, mosaic of hand-cut glazed clay pieces fitted into an interlocking geometric puzzle. Stars with eight, twelve or sixteen points radiate from the centre, surrounded by kite, hexagon and chevron pieces in vibrant glossy colour, cobalt blue, emerald green, saffron yellow, white, black, deep red-brown and turquoise. Edges are crisp with thin pale grout lines, and surfaces are glassy. Borders may carry calligraphic bands or carved plaster. Precise, endlessly repeating tessellations create a jewel-like pattern with no empty space.'),
('Venini Murano Glass','Europe','1940s-50s','glass','In the style of Venini Murano Glass, hand-blown art glass in elegant simple shapes with thin walls and flowing asymmetric curves. Colours are translucent and luminous, emerald green, amber, ruby, aquamarine, lilac and opaque milk white, sometimes layered under clear casing. Techniques create fused patches of colour like woven fabric, delicate lace threads, vertical ribbons, trapped bubbles and acid-etched satin surfaces. Forms feel light, sculptural and playful, with clean mid-century modern lines, polished rims and a tiny etched mark.'),
('Fortuny Textiles','Europe','Early 20th century','textiles','In the style of Fortuny Textiles, velvet and cotton surfaces densely stamped with metallic gold and silver pigment on deep, mottled backgrounds. Colours are rich and muted, aubergine, indigo, burnt sienna, olive, ochre, antique rose and dusty teal, with a hazy, layered, hand-printed look. Patterns are Renaissance pomegranates, arabesques, stylised animals, ogee lattices and medallions repeating in tight rows. A faint shimmer from the metal, a soft aged glow and a velvety hand give a rich, theatrical, exotic mood, with fine pleated folds and softly diffused light.'),
('Gobelins Manufactory','Europe','17th-18th century','textiles','In the style of Gobelins Manufactory, woven wool and silk that looks like painting, built from dense hatching of finely graded coloured threads. Compositions are grand and theatrical, with mythological and historical drama, hanging garlands and architectural frames inside heavy woven borders of acanthus, laurel, fruit and gilt-looking scrollwork. The palette is deep and slightly aged, crimson, royal blue, forest green, ochre, gold and warm brown with pale highlights. Figures are idealised and baroque in pose, with billowing drapery and dramatic clouds. The weave is tight, flat and smooth.'),
('Aubusson Tapestry Workshops','Europe','17th-19th century','textiles','In the style of Aubusson Tapestry Workshops, flat-woven surfaces with no pile and a slightly chalky, dusty, antique hand, in soft pastel-leaning colour. The palette is faded rose pink, powder blue, sage green, cream, pale gold and muted terracotta. Designs use leafy verdure with birds, large acanthus leaves, floral bouquets and romantic medallions framed by woven ribbons, garlands and scrolling cartouches. Compositions feel airy, graceful, curved and symmetrical in a Rococo and Neoclassical manner, with delicate cream borders.'),
('Beauvais Tapestry Manufactory','Europe','18th century','textiles','In the style of Beauvais Tapestry Manufactory, woven surfaces with a playful, pastoral Rococo character. Scenes of gallant romance, children at play and fanciful animals are rendered in soft, sweet colour, pink, cream, sky blue, pale green and gold with brown outlines. Frames of woven flowers, ribbons, scallops and C-shaped scrolls surround each scene. The weave is fine and smooth and the colours glow like painted pastel. Forms are plump with flowing curves, and the mood is light, romantic and decorative.'),
('Savonnerie Manufactory','Europe','17th-18th century','textiles','In the style of Savonnerie Manufactory, thick, plush hand-knotted pile with a velvet surface and a sculptural, monumental pattern. Layouts are formal and symmetrical, with large acanthus scrolls, floral bouquets, shells, garlands and architectural cartouches on a dark ground of deep blue, black, brown or crimson. Motifs are shaded to look three-dimensional, with ivory, gold, rose, sage green and yellow highlights. Wide borders imitate carved mouldings, with fruit and flowers in the corners. The feeling is of a royal palace floor, sumptuous, ordered and heavy.'),
('Sevres Porcelain Manufactory','Europe','18th century','ceramics','In the style of Sevres Porcelain Manufactory, smooth glossy porcelain with deep saturated ground colours, rich royal blue, turquoise, delicate rose pink and apple green, all heavily gilded. Reserved panels hold finely painted cherubs, pastoral figures, birds and flower bouquets in naturalistic colour. Gilt borders, scrolls, beading and lacy foliage trim every edge, and handles curve like branches or flower stems. Forms are swelling, ornate and Rococo. Unglazed matte white parts have a silky, snow-like texture.'),
('Royal Delft','Europe','17th century-present','ceramics','In the style of Royal Delft, creamy tin-glazed white ground with vivid cobalt blue painted decoration, from pale wash to near-black. Scenes of windmills, canals, ships, tulips, villagers and landscapes are drawn with fine brushwork and tonal shading, and small figures sit among curling scrollwork and floral borders. The glaze is shiny, slightly bluish-white and pooled in places. Grids of simple blue motifs repeat with matching corner motifs. The mood is crisp, domestic, nostalgic and traditionally Dutch.'),
('Whitefriars Glass','Europe','1960s-70s','glass','In the style of Whitefriars Glass, heavy, thick-walled studio glass with bold sculptural shapes and a strongly textured surface. Irregular bark-like ridges, chunky bubbles, dimples, moulded ribbing and cased layers of strong colour create a tactile feel. The palette is deep and earthy, tangerine orange, meadow green, kingfisher blue, aubergine purple, ruby red, amber and smoky grey. Forms are cylindrical, tapered, faceted or swirled with heavy bases and slight asymmetry. Surfaces are glossy with a deep glowing inner colour, in a mid-century manner.'),
('Steuben Glass','North America','1930s-50s','glass','In the style of Steuben Glass, crystal clear, flawless, heavy optical glass with pure transparency and thick, polished walls. Shapes are clean, simple and modernist, with crisp curves, tapered columns, deep facets and thick bases. Decoration is minimal, thin wheel-engraved lines of animals and plants, or sharp geometric cuts that refract light. Colour appears rarely, in subtle tints such as pale gold or aqua. The mood is cool, luminous, precise and quiet, like a block of ice shaped by hand.'),
('Gorham Silver','North America','1880s-1900s','silverware','In the style of Gorham Silver, heavy sterling surfaces in a lavish late-Victorian and Art Nouveau manner. Metal is hand-hammered or chased, with bright polished highlights and darker oxidised recesses and a characteristic martelled texture. Decoration includes embossed flowers, lily pads, whiplash curves, dragonflies, Japanese-inspired motifs, inlays of copper and brass in contrasting colour and flowing organic handles. Forms are rounded, with applied leaves and sculpted finials. The feel is rich, sculptural and ornamental, with small stamped marks.'),
('Rookwood Pottery','North America','1880s-1920s','ceramics','In the style of Rookwood Pottery, high-gloss glazed art pottery with softly blurred, naturalistic painted decoration. The signature look has dark backgrounds blending from brown to amber, orange and olive green, with flowers, leaves, fruit and animals painted as if seen through mist. Later looks use matte glazes in soft green, blue and pink, or Art Deco geometry with stylised flowers. Forms are rounded with simple lines, glazes pool and drip, and a small stamped emblem marks the base.'),
('Roycroft Arts and Crafts Community','North America','Early 20th century','decorative arts','In the style of Roycroft Arts and Crafts Community, plain handmade rectilinear construction in oak and hammered copper. Wood has square, sturdy members, exposed through-tenons, flat slats and a warm medium-brown fumed finish with visible grain. Copper shows hammer marks, rivets, strap hinges and a deep brown or warm penny-red patina. Printed matter uses thick handmade paper, red initials, woodcut-like borders, heavy black type and soft suede covers with a stamped orb and cross emblem. The mood is simple, honest, rustic and monastic.'),
('Ntonso Adinkra Cloth Makers','Africa','Traditional-present','textiles','In the style of Ntonso Adinkra Cloth Makers, handwoven cotton stamped with carved stamps in a dark, glossy tree-bark dye. Rows of small, bold black symbols such as concentric circles, spirals, crosses, ladders and hearts repeat inside a grid divided by hand-drawn comb lines. Backgrounds are dyed deep red, brown, indigo or black, or left natural cream. Symbols are slightly irregular with inky edges, giving a handmade rhythm and strong graphic quality. Narrow woven strips are stitched together into a wide cloth.'),
('Hudson Bay Company Point Blankets','North America','18th-20th century','textiles','In the style of Hudson Bay Company Point Blankets, thick, heavy wool in a creamy off-white field with four bold horizontal stripes near each edge, in green, red, yellow and indigo blue. Stripes are flat and evenly spaced, with green and red dominating, and short black tick marks woven into one edge. The cloth has a dense napped, felted surface that feels rustic, warm and hearty, with plainly finished edges and no further decoration. The overall look evokes trading posts, cabins and snow.'),
('Ganado Trading Post Navajo Weavings','North America','1900s-1930s','textiles','In the style of Ganado Trading Post Navajo Weavings, handwoven wool with a deep brick red ground, charcoal black, ivory and soft grey, in strong contrast. A bold central diamond with stepped, serrated edges, or a cross, sits in the centre, with smaller diamonds or hooks echoing in the corners. The border is a single frame with notches or crosses and neat edges. The weave is tight and flat with a slight handspun irregularity. Geometry is crisp, rectilinear and strongly symmetrical, with a warm desert look.'),
('Aldine Press','Europe','1490s-1510s','publishing','In the style of Aldine Press, small, portable, clean and balanced pages set in crisp roman type with open round letters, and a slanted narrow italic with joined strokes. Pages are uncluttered, with generous margins, justified text, simple capitals and almost no illustration. A small printer mark of a dolphin coiled around an anchor appears in black ink on cream laid paper. Covers are soft vellum or brown leather with blind tooling, gilt edges and ties. The look is elegant, humanist, scholarly and compact, with quiet, evenly inked colour.'),
('Plantin Press','Europe','16th century','publishing','In the style of Plantin Press, dense, sturdy Renaissance pages with well-spaced roman type, italic accents and elaborate woodcut or engraved title pages. Title pages are architectural, with columns, pediments, allegorical figures, garlands and cartouches, plus a printer emblem of a hand holding a compass. Black ink sits on warm cream laid paper, with decorative headpieces, ornamented initials and neat marginal notes. Columns in several scripts are symmetrical and orderly. Covers are tooled calf or vellum. The look is rich, rational, ornate and humanist.'),
('Kelmscott Press','Europe','1890s','publishing','In the style of Kelmscott Press, dense Gothic-revival pages in heavy black blackletter type on thick handmade cream paper, with red ink for headings and notes. Each page is framed by luxuriant woodcut borders of winding acanthus, vines, flowers, leaves and fruit, white on black, interlaced thickly. Large foliage initials on a dark ground open each section. Text is tightly justified with minimal leading and wide margins, giving a dark and ornate texture. Covers are limp vellum with silk ties. The mood is medieval, rich and romantic.'),
('Doves Press','Europe','1900s-1910s','publishing','In the style of Doves Press, austere and elegant pages with no illustration and no border. Everything is set in a single classic roman type in rich black ink on thick cream handmade paper, with even word spacing, generous margins and a perfectly balanced column. Only coloured initials in red or blue, drawn with flowing calligraphic flourishes, add ornament, and openings use large simple letters. Title pages are plain with tidy letter spacing. Covers are limp vellum or morocco with a simple gilt line pattern. The mood is pure, calm, disciplined and typographic.'),
('Elzevir Press','Europe','17th century','publishing','In the style of Elzevir Press, tiny pocket-sized pages with neat, crisp roman and italic text set in narrow columns. The title page has an engraved frame or a small printer device of a tree wound with a vine beside an old man. Typography is compact and clear, with small page numbers, ornamental headpieces of printers flowers and small decorated initials. Paper is white to cream, and covers are plain vellum or brown calf with gilt tooling and small marbled endpapers. The mood is compact, practical, tidy and scholarly.'),
('Monotype Corporation','Europe','1920s-30s','typography','In the style of Monotype Corporation, printed matter and specimen sheets showing carefully revived classical roman letterforms and crisp new classics. Text is a fine serif with high contrast, bracketed serifs, balanced proportions and even colour, with a few geometric sans-serif and slab designs. Pages are neat, black ink on cream paper, with rows of alphabets in graduated sizes, small numbers and ornamental rules. Layouts use centred headings, small capitals, letter-spaced capitals and discreet fleurons. The look is orderly, refined, classical and typographic, like a well-printed British book or newspaper.'),
('Letraset','Europe','1960s-70s','graphics','In the style of Letraset, dry-transfer lettering with rows of black or coloured letters on translucent sheets, with a waxy, slightly cracked appearance when rubbed down. Letterforms are bold, expressive 1960s and 70s display faces, chunky rounded letters, psychedelic swirls, inline shadows, stencil cuts, art-nouveau revival and space-age geometry. Sheets are laid out in a neat grid of tiny alphabets and punctuation, with registration marks and a blue guide grid. Applied words are slightly uneven, with tiny gaps and imperfect baselines. The mood is cut-and-paste, punk poster and retro graphic design.'),
('Rotring','Europe','1950s-80s','writing instruments','In the style of Rotring, precision technical-drawing tools with slim hexagonal or round metal bodies in matte black or silver, with a knurled grip and a single red ring near the top. The shape is industrial and tool-like, with a long conical metal nose, a clip and tiny white printed numbers. Colours are black, brushed chrome and a vivid red accent, with colour-coded end caps for sizes. Finishes are crisp and engineered, and the mood is draughtsman, architect studio and exacting accuracy.'),
('Staedtler','Europe','1900s-present','stationery','In the style of Staedtler, functional, tidy German drawing and school goods in a distinctive scheme. Hexagonal bodies are lacquered in bold bands of bright blue and black with a vivid yellow section, or a navy and red set, with gold-printed or white lettering. White erasers are wrapped in blue paper sleeves printed in black and red. Boxes and cases are clean, coloured in blue, black and white with simple sans-serif type. Contrast bands are neat and the lacquer is flat and smooth, with a classroom-like feel.'),
('Faber-Castell','Europe','19th century-present','stationery','In the style of Faber-Castell, a deep dark-green lacquer finish with gold hot-foil stamped lettering and a double-knight emblem. Bodies are hexagonal or round, with a smoothly dipped end in colour and fine grading marks. Tins and boxes are dark green, dark brown or black with gold lettering, with rows of items aligned in gradients. Luxury versions use polished wood with silver or platinum-plated fittings and knurled details. The mood is classic, scholarly, European and craftsmanlike.'),
('Pelikan','Europe','1930s-50s','writing instruments','In the style of Pelikan, plump cigar-shaped bodies in celluloid or resin with a translucent window. Classic versions have a black cap and a green striped, tortoiseshell or black body with gold-coloured trim bands. The clip is a slender arrow or bird-shaped tail, and a gold tip bears fine engraved scrollwork. Later versions appear in bright blue, red and white stripes with a thick, rounded body. Boxes and packaging use deep green and white, with an emblem of a parent bird and chicks. The look is tidy, German, sturdy and luminous.'),
('Montblanc','Europe','1920s-present','writing instruments','In the style of Montblanc, massive cigar-shaped forms in deeply polished black precious resin with gold-plated rings, clip and a large two-tone gold tip. The cap is topped with a white six-pointed star shaped like a snow-capped peak, rounded and domed. Surfaces are glossy and deep black, with thin gold rings and fine engraved numbers. Special versions use striped, marbled or burgundy resin. Cases are black leather with a gold emblem, and accessories follow the same dark and gold luxury. The mood is stately, confident, executive and understated.'),
('Parker Pens','North America','1940s-50s','writing instruments','In the style of Parker Pens, streamlined, slightly tapered cylindrical bodies, bullet-like and sleek. Barrels come in glossy burgundy, blue, black or grey, with fine herringbone, vertical grooves or a smooth finish. Caps are brushed stainless steel or gold-filled, with a ribbed texture and a distinctive arrow-shaped clip that stands proud. The working tip is hooded under a smooth tapered shield. Trim is thin chrome or gold bands. The mood is mid-century post-war optimism with good engineering.'),
('Waterman Pens','Europe','1920s-50s','writing instruments','In the style of Waterman Pens, elegant slim, rounded bodies in black hard rubber, red ripple pattern or sterling silver filigree overlay. Ripple designs swirl in mottled red and black, and metal overlays show Art Deco geometric grooves, basketweave and lacy filigree. Details include thin gold-filled bands, a gently arched clip and fine ornament on the metal tip. Colours are warm, rich and aged, with an orange-red sheen. Boxes are velvet-lined. The mood is refined, Edwardian and Art Deco.'),
('Sheaffer Pens','North America','1930s-50s','writing instruments','In the style of Sheaffer Pens, bulbous, torpedo-shaped forms in glossy striped celluloid colours, pearl and jade green, marine black, grey, burgundy and blue. Thin chrome or gold trim rings circle the body, and a distinctive inlaid white dot sits at the top. A long gold tip has a triangular or hooded shape. Clips are straight, flat and sleek, with a spear-like taper. Surfaces are smooth, rounded, streamlined and glossy, in an Art Deco and early jet-age manner.'),
('Pilot Namiki Maki-e Pens','East Asia','1920s-present','writing instruments','In the style of Pilot Namiki Maki-e Pens, slim forms in glossy black or red lacquer decorated with delicate gold, silver and coloured powder. Tiny cranes, dragonflies, bamboo, plum blossom, waves and mountains appear in raised, sparkling gold with soft gradations, often covering the whole surface. Metal parts are gold or rhodium with fine engraved scrolls. Forms are smooth and elegant with rounded ends, presented in pale paulownia wood boxes. The mood is quiet luxury, patient craft and nature-inspired.'),
('Uni Mitsubishi Pencil','East Asia','1950s-present','stationery','In the style of Uni Mitsubishi Pencil, a deep, mirror-like glossy paint finish in dark burgundy or black, with a single gold foil-stamped line of tiny type and a slim gold band. Hexagonal bodies are smooth and perfectly even, ending in a clean flat end with a thin ferrule. Boxes are slim and rectangular with a window, in dark maroon, black or silver. Clear transparent bodies with coloured grips appear in the more casual range. The overall mood is quiet, luxurious, Japanese, simple and meticulously made.'),
('Lamy','Europe','1960s-80s','writing instruments','In the style of Lamy, modernist, simple and functional design in matte black plastic and brushed stainless steel. Bodies are straight cylinders with a faceted triangular grip section, and a thin sprung wire clip extends along the cap as a single flat bar. Surfaces are matte and fine-grained, with only small dots or windows as detail. Colours are black, charcoal grey, white, and later bright red, yellow and blue. Metal tips are silvery, clean and plain. Packaging is minimal. The mood is German industrial design, school-like and elegantly austere.'),
('Cross Pens','North America','1940s-present','writing instruments','In the style of Cross Pens, slim cylindrical forms with a clean, classical shape and a smooth finish in chrome, brushed gold-filled or lacquer. Surfaces carry a tight diamond-patterned engraving or fine ridged stripes, with a straight narrow clip ending in a tiny ball. Rings are thin and the profile is a perfectly even tube with a rounded twisting top. Colours are silver, gold, black and deep blue. Cases are leather or velvet-lined. The mood is business-like, classic, restrained and subtly formal.'),
('Winsor and Newton','Europe','19th century-present','art supplies','In the style of Winsor and Newton, classic scholarly artists materials in small metal tubes and wooden boxes. Tubes are silver or coloured foil with black or white lettering, a cream label and a bold red or black band, with a colour name and swatch. Mahogany or black japanned tin boxes hold rows of brilliant pigment in neat pans with a brass catch. Handles are long, tapered wood in red, black or natural, with a gleaming crimped metal collar. Dense serif type and ornate Victorian borders give a studious, traditional feeling.'),
('Caran d Ache','Europe','1930s-present','art supplies','In the style of Caran d Ache, a vivid, saturated and carefully graded spectrum in slim metal tins with hinged lids and neat aligned rows. Hexagonal bodies are glossy in a deep colour matching their core, with a white or gold imprint and a smooth round end dipped in the same colour. Tins are silver, red or royal blue with a bright mountain-like or lettering emblem. Metal hexagonal tools have a glossy lacquer and crisp chiselled look. Colours are intense, strong reds, cyans and yellows, in a pristine, orderly, Swiss feel.'),
('Steinway and Sons','North America','1880s-present','musical instruments','In the style of Steinway and Sons, glossy black polished lacquer with a mirror-like curved rim, sweeping asymmetric curved side and a large raised lid. Legs are sturdy, round and tapered, ending in brass casters, with a lyre-shaped gold-toned metal assembly beneath. A gilt metal frame shows within, the name is in gold lettering, and keys are white with ebony sharps. Decoration is elegant and restrained. Alternate finishes are dark mahogany or burl walnut. The mood is grand, opulent, formal and concert-hall dignified.'),
('Bosendorfer','Europe','19th century-present','musical instruments','In the style of Bosendorfer, Viennese grandeur in glossy black or rich warm wood such as walnut, cherry or maple, with an extended bass end and extra black keys. The rim is slender and curved, with a refined and quiet profile. Legs are carved and tapering, and the music desk is lace-like with a shallow ornate shape. Edging is thin, and trim in gold or brass is tasteful. Some versions have elaborate inlay, gilt carving and painted panels in the Baroque and Secession manner. The mood is aristocratic, warm, ornate and old-world.'),
('Selmer Paris','Europe','1930s-50s','musical instruments','In the style of Selmer Paris, elegant curved brass bodies finished in warm honey-gold lacquer or silver plating. Keys are neatly arranged, with pearly rounded buttons, and the flared bell carries fine engraving of swirling floral scrollwork in the Art Deco manner. The neck is long and gently bent, and bells are wide and flared. Tone-hole rims are finely drawn and guards are tidy. Cases are black and plush-lined. The mood is jazz-age, Parisian, tidy and rich in warm gold.'),
('Taylor Guitars','North America','1990s-present','musical instruments','In the style of Taylor Guitars, a modern, clean, sleek look with slim bodies and a cutaway, bright natural-coloured spruce tops, and sides and back in fine-grained sapele, rosewood or koa. Finishes are thin, glossy and flawless, and edges have rounded bevels, especially at the lower arm. Inlays are minimal, slim abalone or ebony markers in an angular, elegant shape, with thin bindings and a dark inlaid ring around the soundhole. Headstocks have a tapered, smooth curve with an engraved logo. The mood is precise, bright, contemporary and carefully finished.'),
('Martin Guitars','North America','1930s-present','musical instruments','In the style of Martin Guitars, classic large, rounded rectangular bodies, a warm golden spruce top that ages to amber, and rosewood or mahogany sides and back. Bindings are cream or ivoroid, and dark herringbone purfling rings the top. The soundhole is circled with fine rosette rings, and the pickguard is tortoiseshell. Fretboards are dark ebony with simple pearl dots, or snowflake inlays on premium versions. The headstock is squared with an inlaid pearl logo. The finish is satin or gloss. The mood is traditional, folksy and rootsy.'),
('Gretsch Guitars','North America','1950s-60s','musical instruments','In the style of Gretsch Guitars, large hollow bodies with a flamboyant orange or deep red-brown lacquer finish, a gold-plated pickguard and bold decoration. Details include twin cutaways, gold sparkle or white binding, a G-shaped cutout, a large V-shaped gold tailpiece and a tall, wide headstock. Hardware is flashy, with chrome, gold, thumbscrew plates and tall knobs. Pickups sit in gold-plated surrounds. Alternate finishes include pearl white, two-tone green and sparkle. The mood is rock and roll, showy, rockabilly and retro.'),
('Rickenbacker','North America','1960s','musical instruments','In the style of Rickenbacker, a distinctive thin semi-acoustic body with glossy red, fireglo sunburst, jetglo black, mapleglo natural or white finish and white plastic binding. Features include a slotted headstock, a sharply pointed cutaway horn on one side, a stylish chrome bridge cover, triangular fretboard inlays, a chrome toaster-shaped pickup and a white pickguard with a tapered contour. Some versions show a rounded cutaway with a curvy edge and a rear control panel with tiny knobs. The mood is jangly, mod, 1960s British Invasion and psychedelic.'),
('Bechstein','Europe','19th century-present','musical instruments','In the style of Bechstein, glossy ebony black with a classic Germanic design. Curved cases have a sharply defined rim, bold Jugendstil carved legs or plain tapering legs, and gold-leaf painted decoration on historic art cases showing Neo-classical or Art Nouveau scenes in muted colours. The lid is large and the music desk is carved. Gold-toned lettering marks the front. Alternative woods include rosewood, walnut and burr maple with warm mellow tones. The mood is cultured, stately, ornate and drawing-room elegant.'),
('Yamaha Pianos','East Asia','1960s-present','musical instruments','In the style of Yamaha Pianos, a flawless gloss black polyester finish with smooth, crisp straight lines and uniform surfaces. Grand shapes have a simple curved rim, a clean music desk and square or rounded legs, with a gold or silver emblem of three crossed tuning forks. Upright shapes are boxy with neat panels, a fold-down lid and plain brass pedals. Alternative finishes include satin ebony, polished mahogany, walnut and white. The mood is modern, tidy, dependable, schoolroom and conservatoire, with perfect reflections.'),
('Bauhaus Weimar Workshops','Europe','1919-1925','design','In the style of Bauhaus Weimar Workshops, expressionist and early constructivist objects made in wood, metal, glass and textile. Forms are built from stacked cubes, slats and planes in primary red, yellow, blue, black and white, with bold geometric shapes such as circle, square and triangle. Woven abstracts are compositions of rectangles and stripes in saturated, earthy reds, blues, ochres and black. Rounded cylindrical forms use nickel or plain glaze. Graphics use heavy sans-serif lettering stacked on grids with diagonal bars. The mood is utopian, experimental, geometric and artisan.'),
('Vkhutemas Workshops','Russia and Eastern Europe','1920s','design','In the style of Vkhutemas Workshops, constructivist stark geometry in red, black, white and cream, with occasional yellow and grey. Compositions place diagonals, circles, wedges and sharp bars at dynamic angles, with heavy angular sans-serif lettering and photomontage. Furniture uses thin plywood, tubing and simple planes with exposed joints. Architecture is stacked boxes, cantilevers, cylinders and glass walls. Prints are flat and high-contrast with rough printed textures. The mood is revolutionary, industrial, energetic and abstract.'),
('De Stijl Workshops','Europe','1917-1930s','design','In the style of De Stijl Workshops, everything built from straight horizontal and vertical lines and rectangles. The palette is limited to pure red, yellow and blue, with white, black and grey, set in flat colour blocks with thick black dividing lines. Objects are assembled from slim rails and flat panels that overlap and project at their ends, painted in contrasting colours with exposed ends. Spaces show asymmetrical balance and open planes. Lettering is blocky and constructed from squares. The mood is strict, rational, abstract and playful in its colour.'),
('Omega Workshops','Europe','1913-1919','design','In the style of Omega Workshops, hand-painted surfaces with bold, loose, post-impressionist decoration. Brush marks are visible, with abstract blobs, stripes, spirals and stylised flowers or figures in strong colours, mustard, magenta, turquoise, orange, lilac, green and black. Compositions are irregular and asymmetrical with an unfinished hand-drawn quality. Underlying forms are plain and painted all over in dense patterns. Glazes are thick and opaque, with spots and swirls. The mood is cheerful, avant-garde, amateurish by design and bohemian.'),
('Guild of Handicraft','Europe','1888-1908','decorative arts','In the style of Guild of Handicraft, Arts and Crafts silver, enamel and jewellery in a hand-wrought manner. Hammered metal shows visible tool marks, curved handles, wire twists and loose, flowing Art Nouveau lines. Peacocks, ships, tendrils, stylised flowers and hearts are set with cabochon moonstones, opals, turquoise or blister pearls and translucent coloured enamel in green, blue and rose. Objects look soft and slightly irregular, with a matte, pale silver surface and delicate suspended drops. The mood is romantic, medieval-influenced, simple and craftsmanlike.'),
('Shaker Mount Lebanon','North America','19th century','furniture','In the style of Shaker Mount Lebanon, plain and sturdy construction in pine, maple and cherry with no ornament. Slender turned posts, arching slats, woven tape or rush seating and tapered legs give light, upright forms. Flat panels, simple pegs, finger joints, small wooden knobs and a smooth surface define the details. Colours are muted milk-paint tones, barn red, mustard yellow, olive green, blue and natural honey wood. Thin bent wood bands with swallowtail joints and copper tacks hold shapes together. Pegboards keep things tidy. The mood is serene, orderly, utilitarian and pure.'),
('Amish Quilt Workshops','North America','Late 19th-early 20th century','textiles','In the style of Amish Quilt Workshops, large simple geometric blocks of solid-colour wool or cotton in deep, saturated tones. The palette is black, plum purple, royal blue, forest green, wine red, brown and dusty rose, with no printed patterns. Designs use a centre diamond, bars, stepped squares and nine-patch grids, framed by wide borders and bound in contrasting colour. Fine, dense stitching creates feathers, wreaths, cables and grids in the dark areas, giving a subtle relief. The mood is bold, austere, modern-looking and graphic.'),
('Gzhel Porcelain','Russia and Eastern Europe','18th century-present','ceramics','In the style of Gzhel Porcelain, bright glossy white ground painted only in cobalt blue brushwork, in graduated tones from pale sky blue to deep navy. Decoration is floral sprigs, leaves, swirling petals, birds, scalloped rims and simple landscapes, painted freely with a wide brush in single-stroke style. Forms are rounded and plump, with soft outlines. The blue appears washed and flowing against the clean white. The mood is folk-like, cheerful, fresh and quintessentially Russian.'),
('Lomonosov Imperial Porcelain Factory','Russia and Eastern Europe','18th-20th century','ceramics','In the style of Lomonosov Imperial Porcelain Factory, bright white porcelain in two manners. The imperial manner shows gilded rims, cobalt blue nets of dark blue lattice with gold dots and rich floral reserves in naturalistic colour, on fine, thin, delicate forms. The revolutionary manner of the 1920s shows suprematist and propaganda designs with sharp red, black and white geometry, stars, workers and slogans in stencilled lettering, filling flat surfaces with boldly angular designs. Surfaces are lively and glossy.'),
('Palekh Lacquer Miniatures','Russia and Eastern Europe','1920s-present','decorative arts','In the style of Palekh Lacquer Miniatures, deep lustrous black glossy ground painted in egg tempera with fairy tale scenes. Elongated, elegant figures with small heads and graceful curved poses move through stylised hills, towers, trees and onion domes. The palette is bright red, gold, emerald green, white and orange against black, with fine gold highlights outlining draperies and ornamental borders of tiny patterns. Interiors are red lacquer. Details are miniature and precise, as in an icon, and the mood is mythical, jewel-like and storybook.'),
]
